rnetes:// workspace store resolve experiment-to-workspace mappings when invoked outside the full tracking server initialization sequence?\n3. Do artifact requests handled by the artifacts-only server (endpoints under /api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/*) carry sufficient identifiers — experiment ID or run ID — to resolve the parent experiment and its workspace assignment, including for the S3 artifact store's {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/ path structure?\n\n## Scope\n\n- Trace the MLflow server initialization sequence for --artifacts-only and for --enable-workspaces individually, and identify what breaks when both are set.\n- Trace the WorkspaceStoreRegistry / mlflow.workspace_provider entry-point loading path in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins and enumerate its initialization dependencies.\n- Determine the artifact request → parent experiment → workspace resolution chain, including how artifact request paths map to experiment IDs for the standard S3 artifact layout; document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n- Exercise the resolution chain locally (server and plugin are pip-installable; workspace store interactions may use test doubles or a lightweight API server).\n- Produce a findings report with a pass/fail answer and evidence for each question.\n- If workspace resolution is coupled to full server initialization, produce a refactoring plan that factors resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Each of the three questions above has a documented pass/fail answer with code-level evidence (file/function references or a reproducible local run).\n- The initialization dependency set of the plugin's workspace resolution is enumerated (what it requires, what it does not).\n- If coupling exists, the refactoring plan identifies the specific initialization path to decouple and the affected plugin surfaces; if no coupling exists, that is documented with evidence.\n- The artifact-path-to-experiment resolution is demonstrated (or its failure documented) for the S3-standard {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/ layout.\n- Limitations for non-standard artifact root configurations are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3 (P0): the answers determine the implementation approach for workspace-validated, fail-closed artifact serving in the MLflow server.\n- Enables HLR-7 (P1): the answer determines whether the auth plugin workspace resolution requires refactoring for the artifacts-only context.\n```\n\nWait — HLR references in the body: \"HLR-1\" etc. are strategy HLR ids, not sibling epic ids — allowed (HLR Traceability section requires it). Good.\n\nE002 body:\n\n```\n# Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nDetermine how the main MLflow tracking deployment should route artifact traffic to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The mechanism choice shapes the operator's HTTPRoute design and decides whether the MLflow tracking server itself needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The investigation has two bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing — HTTPRoute path-based rules direct artifact API traffic to the artifacts-only instance and no tracking-server change is needed; (B) server-side delegation — the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. Under outcome B, an additional implementation work item is created for the tracking-server delegation configuration.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze how the MLflow tracking server currently serves artifact traffic (--serve-artifacts proxy behavior) and what client-visible endpoints carry artifact operations.\n- Determine whether Gateway API HTTPRoute path matching can segregate artifact API traffic from tracking/model registry/prompt registry traffic with the operator's existing conditional CRD discovery pattern.\n- Determine whether the tracking server supports (or needs) a configuration to delegate artifact operations to another base endpoint.\n- Recommend one of the two bounded outcomes with evidence, and specify the routing design implications for the operator's Helm chart templates.\n- Document the decision for the operator split-server topology work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The routing mechanism decision is documented as outcome A or outcome B with evidence from the tracking server's artifact-serving behavior and Gateway API routing capabilities.\n- The decision specifies what the operator must configure on the main tracking deployment and in HTTPRoute resources.\n- If outcome B, the required tracking-server configuration change is specified precisely enough to scope an implementation work item.\n- Client compatibility implications (existing MLflow clients resolving artifact endpoints) are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-6 (P1): determines the mechanism by which the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Informs HLR-5 (P1): shapes the HTTPRoute design for the dedicated instance.\n```\n\nE003 body:\n\n```\n# Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n\n## Description\n\nVerify and extend the kubernetes-auth plugin's workspace resolution so it functions correctly in the MLflow server's artifacts-only context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The plugin, registered through the mlflow.workspace_provider entry point group and loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry, already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking server's non-artifact operations; this epic makes that resolution available to the artifacts-only server. If the workspace resolution feasibility investigation finds the resolution path coupled to full tracking server initialization, this epic refactors it into an independent initialization path within the plugin. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and --app-name kubernetes-auth authentication setup are unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution (workspace store initialization, experiment-to-workspace lookup) against the artifacts-only server context, per the feasibility findings.\n- If coupled to tracking/model registry store initialization, factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path usable when only the workspace provider is initialized.\n- Preserve the plugin's existing behavior for the full tracking server (no regression to non-artifact workspace operations).\n- Unit and local integration tests covering resolution in the artifacts-only context (workspace store accessed without tracking stores).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The plugin resolves experiment-to-workspace mappings in a server context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized, demonstrated by automated tests.\n- Workspace resolution initialization does not require tracking store or model registry store availability.\n- The plugin's existing workspace resolution for the full tracking server is unchanged (regression tests pass).\n- Any refactoring introduced by the feasibility findings is implemented and covered by tests.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 (P1, direct): auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization.\n- Enables HLR-2, HLR-3 (P0): the server's workspace validation and fail-closed denial depend on this resolution capability; priority inherits P0.\n```\n\nE004 body:\n\n```\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMake the MLflow tracking server's --artifacts-only mode composable with the existing --enable-workspaces flag so that artifact serving is validated against workspace membership. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit --workspace-store-uri, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer / SME input). Audit logging of workspace access denials is delivered through server logs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Make --artifacts-only composable with --enable-workspaces without breaking the server initialization sequence for either flag used individually.\n- When both flags are set: require an explicit --workspace-store-uri (fail to start with a clear error if missing), initialize only the workspace provider, keep tracking/model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact endpoints disabled.\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (S3-standard {experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/ layout), look up the experiment's workspace assignment, delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure (no prescribed Kubernetes API mechanics).\n- Fail-closed behavior: deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) when the workspace store is unreachable; deny with HTTP 403 and an insufficient-permissions message when workspace membership validation fails.\n- Backwards compatibility: --artifacts-only without --enable-workspaces retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unaffected.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs (P2 requirement, bundled: it is implemented in the same denial-handling call sites as the 403/503 responses).\n- Measure per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions consistent with existing caching patterns in the tracking server's auth path if the agreed threshold requires it.\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact root configurations.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the server started with --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A is served the artifact (HTTP 200 with correct content).\n- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B is denied with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given the server started with --artifacts-only without --enable-workspaces, any authenticated user is served artifacts without workspace validation (current behavior preserved, HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership).\n- Given workspace-aware artifact serving active and the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- Given --enable-workspaces set without --workspace-store-uri, the server fails to start with a clear error.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in server logs.\n- Automated regression coverage confirms single-tenant/no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior.\n- The per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and documented.\n- Limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): --artifacts-only composable with --enable-workspaces and --workspace-store-uri.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant and non-workspace configurations retain current behavior.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled because it shares the exact denial-handling call sites and code path as the P0 403/503 handling; a separate epic would re-open the same surface and force a second pass, so it is not independently schedulable.\n```\n\nE005 body:\n\n```\n# Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, forming a split-server topology: the main tracking deployment handles experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry, while the dedicated instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation. The operator propagates workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms it already uses for the main tracking deployment, and configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated instance, per the routing mechanism selected by the artifact traffic routing investigation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, following the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply).\n- MLflow CR spec extension to express the split-server artifact serving configuration and its conversion to Helm values.\n- Propagate workspace configuration (--enable-workspaces, --workspace-store-uri, --app-name kubernetes-auth) to the artifacts-only instance using the same mechanisms as the main tracking deployment; the artifacts-only Deployment runs with the flag combination --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts.\n- Configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated instance, per the routing mechanism investigation's outcome (gateway-level HTTPRoute routing and/or tracking-deployment configuration).\n- Gateway API HTTPRoute resources created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar deployed on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment.\n- Operator validation that rejects MLflow CR configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags (misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation).\n- Chart/value plumbing such that the second Deployment requires no structural change to the chart architecture (per strategy assumption; escalate if found false).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciliation creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment (distinct Deployment resources present with the correct flag combinations).\n- The rendered artifacts-only Deployment container command includes --artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts.\n- Artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated instance is configured per the selected routing mechanism and verified in a test cluster (routing verification does not depend on the workspace-aware server capability; any server image may be used).\n- Invalid MLflow CR configurations (artifacts-only instance without workspace flags) are rejected with a clear status/validation error.\n- Existing single-deployment MLflow CRs reconcile unchanged (no regression when split-server configuration is absent).\n- HTTPRoute creation follows the existing conditional CRD discovery pattern.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute.\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n```\n\nBRANCH-B-E006 body:\n\n```\n# Tracking-server artifact-operations delegation configuration\n\n## Description\n\nConditional work item created only if the artifact traffic routing investigation concludes that the MLflow tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance (outcome B). Implements the tracking-server-side delegation configuration — the mechanism by which the main tracking server directs artifact operations to the dedicated instance rather than serving/proxying them itself — as specified by that investigation. If the investigation concludes gateway-level routing suffices (outcome A), this work item does not exist and routing is handled entirely by the operator's HTTPRoute configuration.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement the tracking-server configuration that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the mechanism specified by the routing investigation.\n- Ensure existing MLflow clients remain compatible (artifact endpoint resolution behavior documented and preserved where possible).\n- Configuration is absent/disabled by default; deployments without the split-server topology retain current artifact-serving behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- With the delegation configuration set, artifact operations handled by the main tracking server are directed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Without the delegation configuration, the tracking server's artifact-serving behavior is unchanged.\n- The configuration surface is documented for the operator to manage declaratively.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance (server-side half of the routing mechanism, conditional on outcome B).\n```\n\nWait — E006's title contains no enforcement regex match. ✓. But hmm — check 6 regex: `\\b(lint|static[ -]analysis|CI (check|pipeline|gate|lint)|validation harness|code check)\\b` — none of my titles match. ✓\n\nNote E005 title \"Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator\" — fine.\n\nNow signals files. Format: markdown table with Signal, Value, Rationale columns.\n\nE001 investigation signals:\n- question_specificity: +1 — three concrete questions with definable pass/fail (init dependency enumerated or not; mapping accessible or not; identifiers present or not).\n- source_accessibility: +1 — answers live in readable source: the mlflow repo (red-hat-data-services/mlflow, per mlflow.md) and the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package source; no decision under review.\n- local_runnability: +1 — the server and plugin are pip-installable and runnable locally; init-path and request-parsing questions exercisable locally with the workspace store interface backed by test doubles.\n- cluster_hardware_dependence: 0 — gating questions answerable from source and local runs; confirmatory checks against a live Kubernetes API are peripheral.\n- human_judgment_required: 0 — all questions are technical, MLflow Team-owned; no stakeholder or governance decision gates the answer.\n\nE002 investigation signals:\n- question_specificity: +1 — enumerated decision (outcome A vs B) with defined criteria.\n- source_accessibility: +1 — tracking server artifact-serving behavior readable in mlflow repo; Gateway API HTTPRoute semantics public; operator's existing routing patterns in mlflow-operator repo.\n- local_runnability: 0 — tracking server runs as a pip library, but validating gateway-level routing end-to-end requires a Gateway API implementation (cluster); the decision itself is source/doc-determinable.\n- cluster_hardware_dependence: -1 — peripheral confirmation of the chosen routing mechanism against a live Gateway API implemen