{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-e4d8bec4-e534-432f-a960-c46fbebc50f2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9fe981ca3bab980","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-aqeju_7g/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009 (Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments)\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server artifacts-only topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 | Tracking-server artifact-operations delegation configuration | Implementation (branch B, conditional) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 5 (E001–E005). Branch epic BRANCH-B-E006 is a conditional alternative created only if the routing-mechanism investigation (E002) resolves to outcome B; it is excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: workspace resolution feasibility (P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Investigation: artifact traffic routing mechanism (P1)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Implementation: plugins artifacts-only workspace resolution (P0)\"]\n    E004[\"E004 Implementation: MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only mode (P0)\"]\n    E005[\"E005 Implementation: operator split-server topology (P1)\"]\n    E006[\"BRANCH-B E006 Implementation: tracking-server delegation config (P1, conditional)\"]\n\n    E001 --> E003\n    E001 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n    E002 --> E005\n    E002 -.-> E006\n```\n\nSolid arrows are main-plan edges (dependency must complete before dependent starts). The dashed arrow is a conditional branch edge: BRANCH-B-E006 exists only under outcome B of the E002 investigation.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The feasibility outcome (is plugin workspace resolution coupled to tracking store initialization?) determines whether the plugin work is verification-only or a refactoring — a `rewrite` gate. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Two of the investigation's questions (workspace store accessible without tracking stores; artifact request paths carrying experiment/run identifiers) determine the server's validation approach — a `rewrite` gate. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 12 (framework/library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact-request validation code builds against the plugin's workspace-resolution capability in the artifacts-only context; the capability must exist first. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The routing mechanism outcome determines the operator's HTTPRoute design and main-deployment routing configuration — a `rewrite` gate. |\n| E002 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 / conditional branch | The branch epic exists only if the investigation concludes the tracking server needs a delegation configuration (outcome B). Under outcome A it is removed. |\n| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Parallel roots | Independent unknowns (server/plugin resolution chain vs. operator routing mechanism); no shared deliverable. |\n| No edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no build-against artifact) | The operator's chart templates reference the server's flag combination as configuration, not as an artifact the operator code builds against. The coupling is expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E005 (rendered container command with the correct flags; routing verification does not require the workspace-aware server capability), never as a DAG edge. |\n| No edge BRANCH-B-E006 → E005 | Rule 13 exception (configuration reference) | If outcome B holds, the operator references the delegation configuration the server exposes — a configuration reference consumed at render time, not a build-against artifact; coupling is an AC on E005. |\n\n**Rule 19 (safety-critical) consideration:** the strategy enforces multi-tenant isolation, but the fail-mode and security semantics are already specified in-strategy as testable requirements (fail-closed denial with HTTP 503, HTTP 403 on non-membership, operator rejection of misconfigurations lacking workspace flags). These are carried as acceptance criteria on E004 and E005 rather than re-derived by separate fail-mode/security investigations; the residual unknowns (can resolution be bypassed by unresolvable paths, can it work without stores) are exactly what E001 resolves.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 (enabled by E003) | |\n| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 | |\n| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 | |\n| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to dedicated instance | P1 | E005 (+ BRANCH-B-E006 under outcome B) | |\n| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | P1 | E003 | Priority inherits P0: E003 transitively enables P0 HLR-2/HLR-3. |\n| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | E004 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the audit log entries are emitted from the same denial-handling call sites in the artifact-request validation path that produce the P0 403/503 responses. A separate epic would re-open the identical code surface and force a second pass over the same call sites — not independently schedulable, no separate user value. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n\nThe strategy's Staff Engineer / SME Input is non-empty and takes precedence; the technical approach in the strategy body already reflects all three corrections. Logged for traceability:\n\n1. **No new flag** — the original AI-generated approach proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`; the Staff Engineer direction is to combine the existing `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags. Override applied: E004's scope uses only the existing flag combination; no new server flag or `workspaceAware` CR field appears in any epic.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — override applied: E005 deploys a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute and configures artifact traffic routing, rather than a single CR field with Helm values propagation.\n3. **SSAR detail over-specified** — override applied: epics state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without prescribing Kubernetes API mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview pseudo-resource details); kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` unchanged.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\nThe canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but has no separate entries for `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All epics therefore carry `component: MLflow` (the closest canonical name; `submit.py` silently strips unknown names). Mapping:\n\n- Strategy component **MLflow** → canonical `MLflow` (direct match).\n- Strategy component **MLflow Operator** → canonical `MLflow` (operator epics E002/E005 span this strategy component; noted here per the span rule).\n- Strategy component **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** → canonical `MLflow` (plugin epic E003 spans this strategy component).\n\nAll three strategy components are in the platform per architecture context: `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md` exist under `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/`; `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is covered within `mlflow.md` (external package v1.5.0 loaded via `--app-name kubernetes-auth`). No new-component onboarding chain is required.\n\n## Investigation Gating and Conditional Branches\n\n**E001 — workspace resolution feasibility (P0).** Consolidates three unknowns resolvable by one investigation pass over the same code (mlflow server + plugins): open question \"does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability?\", assumption \"workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without initializing tracking stores\", and assumption \"artifact request paths include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership\". Outcomes do not change which epics exist (the plugin and server epics exist either way) but change their approach — modeled as `rewrite` gates on E003 and E004, not branch files.\n\n**E002 — artifact traffic routing mechanism (P1).** Resolves open question \"how should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated artifacts-only instance?\". Two bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A (gateway-level routing suffices):** no structural change — the main plan applies; E005 implements path-based HTTPRoute routing and no tracking-server change is needed. No branch file.\n- **Outcome B (tracking-server delegation configuration required):** additional epic `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006` (MLflow repo work, which the operator epic cannot own) implements the server-side delegation configuration. It is gated by E002 (`gated_by`, also in `dependencies`) with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if the investigation lands on outcome A, the branch epic is removed.\n\n**Open questions not modeled as epics (with disposition):**\n\n- *Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request* (PM/Engineering) — does not change which epics exist or their structure; the server implementation proceeds identically and caching is a fix-and-retry mitigation. Captured as an acceptance criterion on E004 (measure overhead; short-TTL caching consistent with existing auth-path patterns if the agreed threshold requires it). No latency target exists in the strategy — flagged under Health Warnings.\n- *Audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials* (PM/Security) — HLR-8 already commits to server logs; a channel upgrade changes configuration, not epic structure. Captured as an acceptance criterion on E004; the PM/Security decision is recorded as an open item on that epic's P2 scope.\n\n**Assumptions disposition:** flag composability (implementation work inside E004); workspace-store accessibility and artifact-path resolvability (E001); single-tenant non-affection (regression AC on E004); Helm chart supporting a second Deployment (implementation detail inside E005, escalated as a risk if found false).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **E005 kept above the ~2-week guideline.** Effort estimate: 2–3 sprints (strategy estimate for the operator split-deployment topology). Why not split: it is one logical change — MLflow CR spec → Helm values → rendered artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute plus main-deployment routing configuration; splitting the deployment templates from the routing configuration would duplicate the chart/value plumbing and force two passes over the same chart surface. Conditional re-split trigger: re-split into (a) artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates and (b) artifact traffic routing configuration if the chart template work exceeds the strategy's 2–3 sprint estimate, or immediately if the routing-mechanism investigation lands on outcome B (server-side delegation), which introduces a genuinely distinct configuration surface.\n- **No latency target exists** for the per-request workspace-resolution overhead (NFR defers to an open question owned by PM/Engineering). E004 measures and documents the overhead; the caching decision cannot be made until a threshold is agreed. Scope risk: caching may be required at launch.\n- **Priority note (not an inversion):** the operator split topology (E005) is the largest effort chunk (2–3 sprints) yet P1, while the server work is P0. This is consistent with the HLRs: the P0 requirements are server-level capabilities deliverable and testable without the operator topology; the operator work delivers the P1 deployment-topology requirements.\n- **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** (architecture review, ODH/RHOAI build onboarding, license validation, accelerator/package support, documentation support, UXD support, performance team support). These are launch gates on the strategy, not epic scope; no epic is generated for them. All target repos already have Konflux pipelines per architecture context (recent \"sync pipelineruns with konflux-central\" commits in both mlflow and mlflow-operator), so no onboarding epics are needed.\n- **Audit channel open decision** (PM/Security): HLR-8 commits to server logs; if the decision upgrades the channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), E004's P2 scope grows — watch at refinement.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All four strategy dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy) are internal and exist per the strategy's dependencies table and architecture context; there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The two Investigation epics naturally front-load risk reduction (feasibility and routing design) before the gated implementation work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64914,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae0561e9e7817db7","agent_id":"aa20800bf1cd32bd6","session_id":"56d91365-9d63-408b-ab52-49c7dc5b096b","uuid":"dbbf4c1b-0aa8-42c2-adfa-3c752b637d11","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:44:41.357Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
