{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93259765b99bb366","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n4\tstatus: In Progress\n5\tpriority: Critical\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- refined\n8\tlinks:\n9\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7127'\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## Business Need (from RFE)\n13\t\n14\t> The full business need is maintained in the source RFE: RHAIRFE-7127\n15\t\n16\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n17\t\n18\t### TL;DR\n19\t\n20\tEnable workspace-aware artifact serving by allowing `--artifacts-only` to combine with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag, so the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests against tenant boundaries. The MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, forming a split-server topology where the main tracking server routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance.\n21\t\n22\t**Components**: MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n23\t\n24\t### Technical Approach\n25\t\n26\t#### Combining existing flags for workspace-aware artifact serving\n27\t\n28\tThe MLflow tracking server already supports workspace isolation via `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, with the `kubernetes-auth` plugin from `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` handling workspace-scoped authorization (architecture context: mlflow.md, Dockerfile.konflux CMD). However, `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` cannot currently be combined -- the artifacts-only mode bypasses workspace validation because it serves artifacts based on path alone without resolving workspace context.\n29\t\n30\tThe solution makes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. Tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g., `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests that fail workspace validation receive HTTP 403. No new server flag is introduced -- the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags, consistent with the administrator's intent when they configure both workspace isolation and artifact-only serving (Staff Engineer / SME Input).\n31\t\n32\tThe design is fail-closed: if workspace-aware mode is active but the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation. This ensures transient infrastructure failures do not silently degrade tenant isolation. Single-tenant deployments and deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces` are unaffected -- the artifacts-only proxy retains its current behavior when workspace flags are absent.\n33\t\n34\t#### Split-server operator topology\n35\t\n36\tThe operator change is not a simple configuration toggle. The MLflow operator needs to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, then configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that dedicated instance (Staff Engineer / SME Input). This is a split-server topology: the main tracking server handles experiment tracking, model registry, and prompt registry, while the artifacts-only instance handles artifact serving with workspace validation.\n37\t\n38\tThe MLflow operator already uses a Helm-in-operator pattern where the MLflow CR spec is converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, and applied via Server-Side Apply (architecture context: mlflow-operator.md). The split deployment requires new Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, plus updates to the main tracking Deployment to configure artifact traffic routing. The operator propagates workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms it already uses for the main tracking deployment. Gateway API HTTPRoute resources are created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies (architecture context: mlflow-operator.md, conditional CRD discovery).\n39\t\n40\t#### Auth plugin workspace resolution\n41\t\n42\tThe `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin, registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`, already provides experiment-to-workspace mapping for the tracking server's non-artifact operations (architecture context: mlflow.md). The artifacts-only proxy needs to call the same workspace resolution logic before proxying artifact requests to the storage backend. The key technical work is verifying and extending the plugin so that workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged -- authentication at the ingress level works identically for both the tracking server and the artifacts-only instance (Staff Engineer / SME Input).\n43\t\n44\t### Affected Components\n45\t\n46\t* **MLflow** (MLflow Team): Make `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`; initialize workspace provider without tracking/registry stores; add workspace validation to artifact request handling\n47\t* **MLflow Operator** (MLflow Team): Deploy dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment with Service and HTTPRoute; configure artifact traffic routing from main tracking deployment\n48\t* **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins** (MLflow Team): Verify and extend auth plugin workspace resolution for the artifacts-only server context where tracking stores are uninitialized\n49\t\n50\t### Impacted Teams\n51\t\n52\t* **MLflow Team** (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins): Server-side flag composition, split-server operator topology, plugin workspace resolution in artifacts-only context\n53\t\n54\t### High Level Requirements\n55\t\n56\t* \\[P0\\] `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\n57\t* \\[P0\\] Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\n58\t* \\[P0\\] Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\n59\t* \\[P0\\] Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\n60\t* \\[P1\\] MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute\n61\t* \\[P1\\] Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance\n62\t* \\[P1\\] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\n63\t* \\[P2\\] Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs\n64\t\n65\t### Dependencies\n66\t\n67\t* **Workspace store (kubernetes://)** (internal, exists): Artifacts-only server must resolve experiment-to-workspace mapping via the workspace store; if not configured when `--enable-workspaces` is set, server must fail to start with a clear error\n68\t* **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin** (internal, exists): Auth plugin must support workspace resolution from artifact request paths in a server context where tracking stores are not initialized; may require extension if workspace resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization path\n69\t* **Gateway API** (internal, exists): Operator uses HTTPRoute resources for the artifacts-only instance's ingress; already a conditional dependency with runtime CRD discovery (architecture context: mlflow-operator.md)\n70\t* **kube-rbac-proxy sidecar** (internal, exists): Authentication layer is unchanged; deployed as a sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment\n71\t\n72\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n73\t\n74\t* **Security**: All artifact access in workspace-aware mode must be validated through the existing auth plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`, workspace store), consistent with the tracking server's authorization model (architecture context: mlflow.md). Failure to validate must result in access denial -- fail-closed. Requests with invalid or missing workspace context are denied, not served (RFE: \"artifact access in multi-tenant deployments is properly isolated\").\n75\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: Workspace-aware artifact serving activates only when `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` are both set. Omitting either flag preserves current behavior. No existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning (RFE).\n76\t* **Performance**: Workspace resolution adds a workspace store lookup and auth plugin validation per artifact request. No latency target is specified in the RFE or architecture context -- see Open Questions.\n77\t\n78\t### Out-of-Scope\n79\t\n80\t* Streaming or performance optimization of artifact serving (covered by a separate RFE per the source RFE scope statement)\n81\t* Changes to workspace isolation for non-artifact operations (tracking, model registry, prompt registry)\n82\t* Changes to the workspace model itself (namespace/project mapping, RBAC model, workspace store implementation)\n83\t* Operator changes for shared-PVC or filesystem deployments unrelated to workspace awareness\n84\t* Per-workspace artifact storage isolation at the storage layer (the `MLflowConfig` CR already supports per-workspace S3 credentials via `mlflow-artifact-connection` Secret; this strategy addresses API-level access control, not storage-level partitioning)\n85\t\n86\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed -- requires PM/Engineering validation)\n87\t\n88\t1. Given a multi-tenant MLflow deployment where the artifacts-only instance is started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A through the artifacts-only instance, then the artifact is served successfully, measured by HTTP 200 response with correct artifact content.\n89\t\n90\t1. Given a multi-tenant MLflow deployment where the artifacts-only instance has workspace-aware serving enabled and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B through the artifacts-only instance, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 403 response with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n91\t\n92\t1. Given a deployment where `--artifacts-only` is set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved), measured by HTTP 200 response regardless of workspace membership.\n93\t\n94\t1. Given a multi-tenant deployment with the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied (fail-closed), measured by HTTP 503 response indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n95\t\n96\t1. Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, when the operator reconciles the CR, then the operator creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment, measured by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n97\t\n98\t1. Given the dedicated artifacts-only instance deployed by the operator, the artifacts-only Deployment runs with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, measured by inspecting the container command in the rendered Deployment spec.\n99\t\n100\t### Effort Estimate\n101\t\n102\t**M-L (Medium to Large) -- 4-7 sprints**\n103\t\n104\tThe scope is larger than a standard M due to the split-server operator topology (Staff Engineer / SME Input). Server-side flag composition and workspace validation in the artifacts-only proxy: 1-2 sprints. Auth plugin verification and extension for the artifacts-only context: 1 sprint. Operator split-deployment topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, artifact traffic routing): 2-3 sprints. Integration testing (multi-tenant workspace scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed edge cases, split-server routing): 1-2 sprints. All work is within the MLflow team's components.\n105\t\n106\t### Risks\n107\t\n108\t* **Artifact path resolution complexity**: Artifact paths in the artifacts-only proxy may not straightforwardly map to experiment IDs in all artifact storage backends or custom artifact root configurations. -- _Mitigation_: Scope initial implementation to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration) where artifact paths follow a well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure. Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n109\t* **Auth plugin coupling to full server initialization**: The `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` workspace resolution may be coupled to the tracking server's full initialization path (tracking store, model registry store). If workspace resolution depends on stores that the artifacts-only mode does not initialize, the plugin requires refactoring. -- _Mitigation_: Validate the plugin's initialization dependencies early in sprint 1. If coupled, factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n110\t* **Split-deployment operational complexity**: A split-server topology introduces additional operational surface: two Deployments, two Services, two HTTPRoutes, and artifact traffic routing configuration. Misconfiguration could result in artifact requests bypassing workspace validation. -- _Mitigation_: The operator manages the split topology declaratively, so users configure a single MLflow CR rather than managing individual resources. Operator validation should reject configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags.\n111\t* **Performance impact of per-request workspace resolution**: Each artifact request requires a workspace store lookup and auth plugin validation, adding latency. -- _Mitigation_: Implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path.\n112\t\n113\t### Assumptions\n114\t\n115\t* `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` can be made composable without breaking the server initialization sequence for either flag used individually -- needs validation\n116\t* The workspace store (`kubernetes://` URI scheme) provides experiment-to-workspace mapping that is accessible without initializing tracking stores -- needs validation\n117\t* Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces`, so the artifacts-only proxy retains current behavior when workspace flags are absent -- needs validation\n118\t* The MLflow operator's Helm chart rendering supports addition of a second Deployment resource for the artifacts-only instance without structural changes to the chart architecture -- needs validation\n119\t* Artifact request paths in the `/api/2.0/mlflow/artifacts/*` endpoints include sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership -- needs validation\n120\t\n121\t### Open Questions\n122\t\n123\t* **What is the acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation per artifact request?** _(Owner: PM/Engineering)_ -- Determines whether caching is required at launch or can be deferred to a follow-up\n124\t* **How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated artifacts-only instance?** _(Owner: MLflow Team)_ -- Affects HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations\n125\t* **Does the workspace store initialization in \\`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\\` depend on tracking store availability?** _(Owner: MLflow Team)_ -- Determines scope of plugin refactoring needed for the artifacts-only context\n126\t* **Should workspace access denials generate audit events through a specific telemetry channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log, server log)?** _(Owner: PM/Security)_ -- Determines scope of audit logging requirement\n127\t\n128\t### Supporting Documentation\n129\t\n130\t* Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7127\n131\t* Architecture context: mlflow.md (RHOAI 3.6-ea.1), mlflow-operator.md (RHOAI 3.6-ea.1)\n132\t* Overlay considered: 0002 -- Go SDK available for MLflow (workspace isolation support in Go clients; tangential to this strategy's server-side scope)\n133\t* Design doc: _To be created_\n134\t* ADR: _To be created if needed_\n135\t\n136\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n137\t\n138\t* **Architecture Review**: _Pending human review_\n139\t* **ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding**: _Pending human review_\n140\t* **Licence Validation**: _Pending human review_\n141\t* **Accelerator/Package Support**: _Pending human review_\n142\t* **Documentation Support**: _Pending human review_\n143\t* **UXD Support**: _Pending human review_\n144\t* **Performance Team Support**: _Pending human review_\n145\t\n146\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n147\t\n148\t_Add technical corrections, architectural direction, component preferences, or domain expertise below. Write in declarative, cumulative form -- statements that remain valid across refinement iterations. This input takes priority over architecture context when they conflict. After review: address findings, then remove the needs-attention label from Jira._\n149\t\n150\t### No new flag -- combine existing flags\n151\t\n152\tThe STRAT proposes a new `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and a corresponding `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field. This is wrong. The correct approach is to allow `--artifacts-only` to be combined with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag. When both are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. Tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact APIs stay disabled. No new server flag is needed. Remove all references to `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware`.\n153\t\n154\t### Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle\n155\t\n156\tThe STRAT describes the operator change as a simple CR field and Helm values propagation. The actual scope is larger: the operator needs to deploy a **dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance** as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, then configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that instance. This is a split-server topology, not a single-deployment configuration flag.\n157\t\n158\t### SSAR detail is over-specified\n159\t\n160\tThe STRAT prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview against `mlflow.kubeflow.org` pseudo-resources throughout the technical approach. This is an mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation detail, not strategy-level. The strategy should state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n161\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0e03bcdde289170","agent_id":"ae9b5f452b559db63","session_id":"56d91365-9d63-408b-ab52-49c7dc5b096b","uuid":"fd3ab7ac-1195-4ed0-a4f7-0f0b76831f37","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:47:44.546Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-93259765b99bb366","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
