{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4abbeef46ac3c47","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n3\ttitle: Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Medium\n19\tai_implementability_score: 1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Investigate artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tDetermine how the main MLflow tracking deployment should route artifact traffic to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The mechanism choice shapes the operator's HTTPRoute design and decides whether the MLflow tracking server itself needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The investigation has two bounded outcomes: (A) gateway-level routing — HTTPRoute path-based rules direct artifact API traffic to the artifacts-only instance and no tracking-server change is needed; (B) server-side delegation — the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance, which creates an additional tracking-server implementation work item.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Analyze how the MLflow tracking server currently serves artifact traffic (the `--serve-artifacts` proxy behavior) and which client-visible endpoints carry artifact operations.\n34\t- Determine whether Gateway API HTTPRoute path matching can segregate artifact API traffic from tracking, model registry, and prompt registry traffic, consistent with the operator's existing conditional CRD discovery pattern for Gateway API.\n35\t- Determine whether the tracking server supports (or needs) a configuration to delegate artifact operations to another instance.\n36\t- Recommend one of the two bounded outcomes with evidence, and specify the routing design implications for the operator's Helm chart templates and main-deployment configuration.\n37\t- Document the decision, including client compatibility implications (how existing MLflow clients resolve artifact endpoints under each outcome).\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- The routing mechanism decision is documented as outcome A or outcome B with evidence from the tracking server's artifact-serving behavior and Gateway API routing capabilities.\n42\t- The decision specifies what the operator must configure on the main tracking deployment and in HTTPRoute resources.\n43\t- If outcome B, the required tracking-server configuration change is specified precisely enough to scope a tracking-server implementation work item.\n44\t- Client compatibility implications for existing MLflow clients are documented for the selected outcome.\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\t- Enables HLR-6 (P1): determines the mechanism by which the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n49\t- Informs HLR-5 (P1): shapes the HTTPRoute design for the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0e03bcdde289170","agent_id":"ae9b5f452b559db63","session_id":"56d91365-9d63-408b-ab52-49c7dc5b096b","uuid":"12ba5f92-ef5b-4f40-a2a1-061cc4d4693e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:47:48.905Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4abbeef46ac3c47","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
