{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-81f07ca86cb972e3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If artifact request paths do not carry sufficient identifiers\n16\t    or the workspace store is inaccessible without tracking stores, the server-side\n17\t    validation and initialization approach is redesigned.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 4\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tMake the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag so that artifact serving is validated against workspace membership. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores and model registry stores uninitialized with non-artifact API endpoints disabled. For each artifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer / SME input). Audit logging of workspace access denials is delivered through server logs.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Make `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` without breaking the server initialization sequence for either flag used individually.\n44\t- When both flags are set: require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (the server fails to start with a clear error if it is missing), initialize only the workspace provider, keep tracking and model registry stores uninitialized, and keep non-artifact API endpoints disabled.\n45\t- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (S3-standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — reusing that infrastructure without prescribing its internal Kubernetes API mechanics.\n46\t- Fail-closed behavior: deny artifact requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable when the workspace store is unreachable; deny with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions when workspace membership validation fails.\n47\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unaffected.\n48\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs (P2 requirement, bundled: the log entries are emitted from the same denial-handling call sites as the 403/503 responses).\n49\t- Measure the per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path, if the agreed threshold requires it.\n50\t- Document limitations for non-standard artifact root configurations.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A is served the artifact, measured by HTTP 200 response with correct artifact content.\n55\t- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B is denied, measured by HTTP 403 response with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n56\t- Given the server started with `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user is served artifacts without workspace validation (current behavior preserved), measured by HTTP 200 response regardless of workspace membership.\n57\t- Given workspace-aware artifact serving active and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied (fail-closed), measured by HTTP 503 response indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n58\t- Given `--enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error.\n59\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in server logs.\n60\t- Automated regression coverage confirms single-tenant and no-workspace-flag configurations retain current behavior.\n61\t- The per-request latency overhead of workspace resolution is measured and documented.\n62\t- Limitations for non-standard artifact root configurations are documented.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability\n65\t\n66\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n67\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403.\n68\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n69\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior.\n70\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled because it shares the exact denial-handling call sites and code path as the P0 403/503 handling in the artifact request validation surface; a separate epic would re-open the same surface and force a second pass over the same call sites, so it is not independently schedulable.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0e03bcdde289170","agent_id":"ae9b5f452b559db63","session_id":"56d91365-9d63-408b-ab52-49c7dc5b096b","uuid":"57bc7efa-b72c-40db-bd40-f089cfdec3bf","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:47:50.207Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-81f07ca86cb972e3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
